# Text is LF in the repository AND in the working tree, on every platform.
#
# SRI pins are SHA-384 over the bytes the server actually sends. A Windows
# checkout with core.autocrlf=true serves CRLF for a file whose committed blob
# is LF, so a pin computed over the blob fails in the browser (`style.css` was
# blocked outright by this) while a pin computed over the working tree would
# fail on the next Linux checkout. `eol=lf` makes the two the same bytes.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary artefacts must never be line-ending translated. With core.autocrlf=true
# on Windows checkouts, PDFs without this attribute were CRLF-smudged on
# checkout, shifting every byte offset after the first LF and breaking the
# byte-exact recovery fixtures (merge_recovery: outlines_for_editor.pdf).
*.pdf binary
*.png binary
*.ppm binary

# Fuzz seeds are libFuzzer corpus inputs: byte-exact, never EOL-translated
# (SL-1.ROB.02).
fuzz/seeds/** -text

# DER test fixtures (SL-1.ENC.03 PKCS#8 keys) are byte-exact binaries.
*.der binary

# Compiled WebAssembly and font payloads are bytes, not text. `text=auto` would
# usually sniff these correctly; naming them makes it a rule rather than a
# heuristic that a future compression change could flip.
*.wasm binary
*.woff2 binary
